Subject: propagate an interrupted finalizer instead of warning

'GCTM' turns any error a __gc metamethod raises into a warning through
'luaE_warnerror' and carries on. That is right for a finalizer that
merely failed, and wrong for one the sandbox stopped: it would let a
finalizer both outlive its CPU limit and hide that it had.

The test is on the atomic interrupt FLAG rather than on the error
object, deliberately. Inspecting the object means Lua stack calls that
can allocate, and this code runs inside the collector; one relaxed load
answers the question with no interpreter involvement at all. It is also
the only test that still works for an error a __gc metamethod raised
itself after the limit had already tripped.

'L->errorJmp' is checked because luaD_throw with no handler aborts the
process. The only path here without one is the finalizer sweep inside
lua_close(), where the extension has already cleared the flag -- but
"would abort" is not a thing to leave to an ordering documented
elsewhere.

The throw abandons the rest of the current collector step. The object
being finalized has already left 'tobefnz' and both 'g->gcstp' and
'L->allowhook' are restored before this point, so the remaining
finalizers simply stay pending and run in a later step.

Behind LUAEXT_LUA_HOOKS, so a build without it is byte-identical to
stock upstream Lua.

diff --color -Naur a/src/lgc.c b/src/lgc.c
--- a/src/lgc.c	2026-08-23 15:40:17
+++ b/src/lgc.c	2026-08-23 15:40:17
@@ -1001,6 +1001,37 @@
     L->allowhook = oldah;  /* restore hooks */
     g->gcstp = oldgcstp;  /* restore state */
     if (l_unlikely(status != LUA_OK)) {  /* error while running __gc? */
+#if LUAEXT_LUA_HOOKS
+      /*
+      ** Turning an error into a warning is right for a finalizer that
+      ** merely failed, and wrong for one that was stopped by the sandbox:
+      ** swallowing that would let a __gc metamethod both outlive its CPU
+      ** limit and hide the fact.
+      **
+      ** The test is on the atomic interrupt FLAG, deliberately, and not on
+      ** the error object. Inspecting the object means Lua stack calls that
+      ** can allocate, and this runs inside the collector. One relaxed load
+      ** answers the question with no interpreter involvement at all.
+      **
+      ** 'errorJmp' is checked because luaD_throw with no handler aborts the
+      ** process. The only way to reach here without one is the finalizer
+      ** sweep inside lua_close(), where the extension has already cleared
+      ** the flag -- but "would abort" is not a thing to leave to a
+      ** documented ordering somewhere else.
+      **
+      ** Note what is NOT restored on this path: g->gcstp and L->allowhook
+      ** already were, three lines up. What the throw does abandon is the
+      ** rest of the current collector step; the object being finalized has
+      ** already left 'tobefnz', so the remaining finalizers stay pending
+      ** and run in a later step.
+      */
+      luaext_irq *luaext_q = LUAEXT_IRQ(L);
+      if (luaext_q != NULL && L->errorJmp != NULL &&
+          atomic_load_explicit(&luaext_q->interrupted, memory_order_relaxed)) {
+        atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_acquire);
+        luaD_throw(L, status);  /* propagate instead of warning */
+      }
+#endif
       luaE_warnerror(L, "__gc");
       L->top.p--;  /* pops error object */
     }
